  ('class7-science-en-chunk-3-005', 'class7-science-en', 'class7-science-en-ch3', 'en', 28, 29, 33, 34, 5, 'Sundori, Goran etc. =. 9 evel pores: a . in respraton Reproductive Roots: Roots of some plants take (© efit we fl ee spart in reproduction, such as sweet potatoes. = fa ka : = : p15 > Xo a N Fig- 3.14 : Pneumatophor Ss External morphology of plants 29 Lessons- 5 — 7: Modified Stems We know that the stems usually grow above the soil level ) \ g \ Ves and bear leaves, flowers and fruits. But in exceptional G i occasions for performing few special functions, stems | ap change their position and structure. These changes are called modifications of stems. Based on position the stems V are of three types, such as — 1. Underground modified stem E 2.Subaerial modified stem and 3. Aerial modified stem . reproduchive /qtoot of teasle Underground modified stems: To survive from adverse i gourd (Kakrol) conditions, storage of food and vegetative propagation, fo") stems of few plants grow beneath the soil surface. These / ; are underground modified stems. They are of four types, Fig-3.15: Resodoctive root such as Tuber, Rhizome, Bulb and Corm. Tuber: Potatoes isan example of tuber. Tubers fo» bear nodes, inter nodes, scale leaves and auxiliary \f . oa scale eal buds. Cavities at the leaf axils of scale leaves are called | eae & Eye ‘eyes’. In favourable conditions, axillary buds grow lg ‘a j from ‘eyes’ and give rise to new plants. Tubers are Co round and swollen due to storage of food. gl Fig- 3.16: Tuber Rhizomes: Stems of Ginger, Turmeric etc. plants are ma pe examples of rhizome. They grow parallel to soil surface AY jl zs Ri de or erect and store foods. It bears clear nodes and W) iif Cay internodes. Scale leaves and roots at nodes, and buds at ?', 302, '09-Science.pdf', 'tesseract 5.3.4 -l eng --psm 6 (pdftoppm -gray -r 200)', 'Raw tesseract OCR; equations, chemical symbols, units, diagram/graph labels do NOT survive OCR reliably — never quote OCR equations/symbols a