  ('class7-science-en-chunk-3-006', 'class7-science-en', 'class7-science-en-ch3', 'en', 29, 30, 34, 35, 6, 'surface AY jl zs Ri de or erect and store foods. It bears clear nodes and W) iif Cay internodes. Scale leaves and roots at nodes, and buds at ? yey 2» the axils of scale leaves are found. fv nt ‘ Fig- 3.17: Raizome Bulbs: Stems of onion, garlic etc. are bulbs. Their stems jy sca leaves, dh aera 2ud are very small and round and convex (elevated at the Yi middle). Nodes and internodes are compressed. Thick NE and juicy scale leaves are so arranged that the bulb sten Ye... bad < cannot be seen. This stem produces profuse fibrous roots. i A) aS Fig- 3.18: Bulb 30 Science Corms: Amorphophallus (B.ol) is an example of \ Be oe ral corm. This stem is very big and more or less round. C) ed, It bears prominent nodes and internodes. Buds from Yi. ¥ Je \\ the axils of scale leaves, grow laterally and give rise to “§/ . C ry iS daughter corm. Afterwards, new plants grow through A 1 Oe ee trow the buds. Fig- 3.19: Corm Sub Aerial Modified Stems : Plants,with soft stems (herbaceous stems), give rise to special branches. These branches produce new plants through vegetative propagation. Some weak stems lying on or under the soil surface are called sub aerial modified stems. They are of four types. Runner: From the axiliary buds of lower nodes of Hydrocotyle itm gp, (B. Thankuni), Oxalis (B. Amruli), Cynodon (Durba) plants give aa? “7 rise to some weak stems called runner. | & Stolon:These are special types of runners. From the base of WZ = Arum (Man Kochu) grows some horizontal long branches. Nodes ny nner of the branches give rise to some roots which are attachd with the —_-Fig-3.20: Runner soil and the rest of the branch is a bit curved.', 305, '09-Science.pdf', 'tesseract 5.3.4 -l eng --psm 6 (pdftoppm -gray -r 200)', 'Raw tesseract OCR; equations, chemical symbols, units, diagram/graph labels do NOT survive OCR reliably — never quote OCR equations/symbols as exact.'),
  ('class7-science-en-chunk-3-007', 'class7-science-en', 'class7-science-en-ch3', 'en', 30, 31, 35, 36, 7, 'branches. Nodes ny nner of the branches give rise to some roots which are attachd with the —_-Fig-3.20: Runner soil and the rest of the branch is a bit curved. Afterwords, new plants grow from the buds. LN oA AN DEIN IR FD Offset: Inter nodes of some aquatic plants like Pistia (B: a i} /} . Teropana) and Eicchornia (B. Kochuripana)are thick and short, y/ so the stems look dwarf. These are called offset. eS = - Sucker: Axiliary buds of Chrysenthemum (B. Chandramallika), Fig- 3.21 - stolol Bamboo (B. Bansh) grow laterally beneath the soil surface and produce new plants. : tes Aerial Modified Stems: ou DQ These stems remain above the soil like normal stems but = ARE cer they are modified to perform special functions like ~~~~= ~~~ synthesis of food, vegetative reproduction, self defense, Fig-3.22: Ofset climbing etc. They are of four types. Phylloclade: Cactus (B. Phonimonosha) is the example a Ze Yip of phylloclade. They are flat and green like leaves and yi Caw c can manufacture food. Leaves of this stem are modified “2 8F EN to spines and are used for self defense. Fig- 3.23: Sucker 5 External morphology of plants 31 Thorn: Sometimes axillary buds are modified into stout and sharp thorns instead of developing as leaf buds. In wood apple (B. Bel), Lawsonia (B. Mehendi), Maina Kata etc. plants develop sharp thorns. bulbil Phe — seins leaf J ‘i By Pe D> ; ei bf aie ae) A, IW CN) i PSZ. Ai XJ] * ‘ lociad ENA SO X fr is and phyllocl le > <p = ‘ \f | Cy Amen) Sit VAS See ia i/ Mi Fig-3.24:Phylloclade —_ Fig- 3.25: Stem tendrils Fig-3.26: Thorns Fig-3.27: Bulbil Stem tendril: Passion flower, Harjora etc.climbers develop thread like thin and spiral long structures which are called stem tendrils.', 308, '09-Science.pdf', 'tesseract 5.3.4 -l eng --psm 6 (pdftoppm -gray -r 200)', 'Raw tesseract OCR; equations, chemical symbols, units, diagram/graph labels do NOT survive 